C = [
("h1", "11. Objections and Replies"),
("p", "Claim: eight objections stand against the position, seven from modern writers who hold that the sign gifts continue and one from modern writers who hold that they ended and date the end wrongly. Each objection is stated, set against its passage and ruled on. Each ruling names the passage at which the opposing reading fails and the point at which it crosses the boundary between Israel’s program and the Body. The volume names each writer and the work in which he states his case, states the position in its own words and puts no quotation marks around any sentence of any writer."),
("sub", "11.1 Who is named, and for what"),
("p", "Seven writers hold that the miraculous gifts continue. Gordon D. Fee, in //God’s Empowering Presence//, reads Paul’s letters as the record of the Spirit’s presence among the churches in the last days, and reads the gifts of 1 Corinthians 12–14 as that presence at work in the gathered church, expected by Paul to continue until the end of the age. Craig S. Keener, in //Miracles: The Credibility of the New Testament Accounts//, argues that the miracle reports of the Gospels and Acts are historically credible, that a large body of comparable reports exists from later centuries and from the present, and that a rule excluding miracles in advance cannot be defended. Sam Storms, in //Practicing the Power//, holds that every gift of the New Testament continues, prophecy, tongues and healing among them, and answers the arguments for cessation from the apostles and from 1 Corinthians 13. Wayne Grudem, in //Systematic Theology// and in the volume he edited, //Are Miraculous Gifts for Today?//, holds that the office of apostle ended and the gifts did not, and holds that prophecy in the New Testament churches is a report of something God brought to mind, open to error, and not the word of God in the sense that Scripture is."),
("p", "John Wimber, in //Power Healing//, holds that healing and other signs go with the proclamation of the kingdom and belong to the ordinary witness of the church. Jack Deere, in //Surprised by the Power of the Spirit//, writes as a former cessationist and argues that Scripture nowhere says the miraculous gifts would cease and that the arguments from the purpose of signs fail. Michael L. Brown, in //Authentic Fire//, defends the continuation of the gifts in reply to John MacArthur and argues that the New Testament gives them no date of ending. The volume rules against all seven on the passages that follow. Each of the seven carries the signs of Israel’s program into the Body, and each carries them past the record that ends them."),
("p", "Four writers hold that the miraculous gifts ended. Richard B. Gaffin Jr., in //Perspectives on Pentecost//, argues that the miraculous gifts were tied to the apostolic and revelatory period of the church. John F. MacArthur Jr., in //Strange Fire//, holds that the sign gifts ended with the apostolic age and served to confirm the apostles and their message. Robert L. Thomas, in //Understanding Spiritual Gifts//, a verse-by-verse study of 1 Corinthians 12–14, argues that tongues and prophecy ceased and that modern charismatic practice does not match the gifts of the New Testament. Thomas R. Schreiner, in //Spiritual Gifts//, holds a qualified cessationism: the apostolic and prophetic gifts ended because the foundation of the church was laid (Ephesians 2:20), and the prophecy and tongues claimed today are not the gifts the New Testament describes. The volume rules with the four that the miraculous gifts ended, and rules against them on the ground and the date (11.9)."),
("p", "D. A. Carson, in //Showing the Spirit//, reads 1 Corinthians 12–14 closely and holds that “that which is perfect” is the return of Christ, so that the passage does not prove the gifts ceased; he also holds that claims to prophecy must be tested. The volume rules against his reading of 1 Corinthians 13:10 (11.8) and does not count him among those who hold that the gifts ended."),
("sub", "11.2 Objection one: Mark 16 says the signs follow believers"),
("p", "Wimber and Brown read Mark 16:17, “And these signs shall follow them that believe,” as the standing pattern of the church’s witness in every age. The commission is spoken to the eleven (Mark 16:14, 15), and the closing verse reports what followed: “they went forth, and preached every where, the Lord working with them, and confirming the word with signs following” (Mark 16:20). The tense of the report is past. The promise runs on in the next verse: “They shall take up serpents; and if they drink any deadly thing, it shall not hurt them; they shall lay hands on the sick, and they shall recover” (Mark 16:18). The reading takes the first clause as a standing promise and leaves the rest without a ground. The textual question of the passage belongs to Study 25."),
("p", "**Where the reading breaks the boundary.** The commission belongs to the eleven, the messengers of Israel’s program, and the signs are the confirmation of their word. The reading lifts a commission given to Israel’s messengers and sets it as the ordinary witness of the Body, and so carries Israel’s confirming signs across the boundary with no passage that moves them. **Verdict.** This reading fails at the passage. The commission names the eleven, verse 20 reports the confirmation in the past tense, and verse 17 cannot be separated from verse 18."),
("sub", "11.3 Objection two: Joel says the Spirit is poured on all flesh"),
("p", "Storms and Fee read Joel 2:28, “I will pour out my spirit upon all flesh,” and Peter’s quotation of it at Pentecost, as the opening of the last days as an age of the Spirit’s empowering presence for the whole church. The passage continues in Joel to name Zion: “for in mount Zion and in Jerusalem shall be deliverance, as the LORD hath said, and in the remnant whom the LORD shall call” (Joel 2:32). Peter introduces the quotation to the men of Israel (Acts 2:22) and cites the wonders as a sign “before that great and notable day of the Lord come” (Acts 2:20). The same quotation carries wonders that Acts 2 does not report: “The sun shall be turned into darkness, and the moon into blood” (Acts 2:20). The coming of the Spirit at Pentecost belongs to Acts Commentary Volume 2."),
("p", "**Where the reading breaks the boundary.** Joel names Zion and the remnant, and Peter speaks to Israel. The reading takes Israel’s last-days promise, tied to a day still named as coming, and sets it as the charter of the Body. **Verdict.** This reading fails at Joel 2:32 and at Acts 2:20. The audience is Israel, the day is the day of the Lord, and the quotation joins the pouring out to wonders the chapter does not record."),
("sub", "11.4 Objection three: Hebrews says the gifts are “according to his own will”"),
("p", "Deere and Grudem answer the argument that signs authenticated the first messengers and then stopped, and Deere reads Hebrews 2:4, “God also bearing them witness, both with signs and wonders, and with divers miracles, and gifts of the Holy Ghost, according to his own will,” as a statement that God distributes such gifts at His pleasure in every age. The clause “according to his own will” is in the verse. The verse stands in a sentence whose verbs are past: the salvation “began to be spoken by the Lord, and was confirmed unto us by them that heard him.” The writer addresses Hebrew readers about the confirmation that came to them through the first hearers."),
("p", "**Where the reading breaks the boundary.** The letter is written to Hebrews about a witness that reached them second-hand. The reading takes a statement made to Hebrews about a past confirmation and turns it into a charter for gifts in the Body in every age. **Verdict.** This argument strains at the sentence and does not survive it. The verbs are past, the readers are Hebrews, and “according to his own will” governs the witness the sentence describes and states nothing of future repetition."),
("sub", "11.5 Objection four: John says the signs are written that we might believe"),
("p", "Keener argues that the sign accounts of the Gospels are credible testimony and that comparable reports from other centuries stand beside them. The volume sets the argument beside John 20:30, 31: “many other signs truly did Jesus in the presence of his disciples, which are not written in this book: But these are written, that ye might believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God.” The signs were done “in the presence of his disciples,” and it is the writing of them that serves belief. The text does not say that the signs will be done again for the reader. It says that the book carries the signs it selected."),
("p", "**Where the reading breaks the boundary.** John’s signs were done before Israel for the confession that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and the book is written to that end. The argument turns a written selection of Israel’s signs into evidence for a standing program of signs in the Body. **Verdict.** This argument strains at the stated purpose. The credibility of the accounts is a question of history; whether a sign-program is in force is a question of what the passages say a sign is, a thing a sender gives a hearer to confirm a word. Keener answers the first question and leaves the second untouched."),
("sub", "11.6 Objection five: Scripture does not announce an end"),
("p", "Deere, Brown and Storms hold that no verse says the gifts would cease, and that the one who claims an end carries the burden of showing it. The first clause is correct: no verse of Acts says that signs have ceased. The volume accepts the burden and carries it on three observations of the text. The last sign recorded is in Acts 28:8, 9. The same chapter records a verdict on the leaders of Israel and the sending of the salvation of God to the Gentiles (Acts 28:25–28). And the book’s last two verses report two years of preaching and teaching with no sign. The same standard binds the objection. No verse says that the signs continue unbroken to the present, and the objection reaches that conclusion from the silence it faults. Paul’s command to the Corinthians, “covet to prophesy, and forbid not to speak with tongues” (1 Corinthians 14:39), was written from Ephesus (1 Corinthians 16:8) within the years Acts records, before the close of the book."),
("p", "**Where the reading breaks the boundary.** The objection carries the sign-program of Israel’s record across the suspension at Acts 28 and into the Mystery Program with no passage that does it. Signs do not continue into the Mystery Program, and a silence cannot move them there. **Verdict.** This argument cannot stand in the text. It rests on silence, it places on the other side a burden it does not carry itself, and the one command it can cite was written before the book closes. The objection must show a command or a report from after Acts 28, and it shows none. Volume 2 reads Paul’s instructions on tongues and prophecy in order. Acts Commentary Volume 5 argues the suspension itself."),
("sub", "11.7 Objection six: believers report healings today"),
("p", "Keener collects reports of healing from the present and from past centuries, and Wimber and Brown point to healing in prayer in the churches they know. The position makes no statement about God’s power. It does not deny that God can heal or that believers may pray for the sick. It does not say what He does in answer in any age. A sign in the passages carries four marks: a sender, a hearer, a word confirmed and an effect no one disputes. The lame man at the gate “immediately his feet and ankle bones received strength” (Acts 3:7), and the council says of him, “a notable miracle hath been done by them is manifest to all them that dwell in Jerusalem; and we cannot deny it” (Acts 4:16)."),
("p", "**Where the reading breaks the boundary.** The objection names recoveries after prayer as Kingdom signs and so continues Israel’s sign-program inside the Body. A report of recovery is testimony to be weighed as testimony. It has no sender who confirms a messenger’s word before the nation and no hearer addressed. **Verdict.** This reply fails at the definition of a sign. The reports are not signs in the sense of Acts 3:7 and Acts 4:16, and the volume does not take them as evidence either way. The passages that bear on prayer for the sick in the letters to the Body are read in Volume 3."),
("sub", "11.8 Objection seven: “that which is perfect” is the return of Christ"),
("p", "Fee, Grudem, Storms and Carson read 1 Corinthians 13:10 as naming the return of Christ, or the consummation, and conclude that the gifts of 13:8 stand until then. The volume reports the verses as written: “whether there be prophecies, they shall fail; whether there be tongues, they shall cease; whether there be knowledge, it shall vanish away” (1 Corinthians 13:8), and “But when that which is perfect is come, then that which is in part shall be done away” (1 Corinthians 13:10). The volume does not identify “that which is perfect,” and the ruling below does not depend on identifying it. The passage states that these gifts end and states what ends them."),
("p", "**Where the reading breaks the boundary.** The reading takes a sentence written within the years Acts records and makes it the promise that the gifts run unbroken past the close of those years. The verse does not speak of Acts 28 and does not speak of the boundary, and the reading carries the gifts across it on that silence. **Verdict.** This inference cannot stand in the verse. The verse states an end; it does not state that none comes earlier and does not state the order in which the gifts end. The objection moves from a stated end to a claim of unbroken continuance, and the second step is not in the text. The ruling rests on the record of Acts 28 and on what the passages say the signs were for. Volume 2 reads 1 Corinthians 12–14 in order."),
("sub", "11.9 Objection eight: the signs ended with the apostles, not at Acts 28"),
("p", "Gaffin holds that the gifts of revelation and miracle belong to the foundation of the church and not to its continuing life. MacArthur holds that the sign gifts ended with the apostolic age. Thomas holds that tongues and prophecy ceased, reading 1 Corinthians 12–14 verse by verse. Schreiner grounds the end of the apostolic and prophetic gifts in the laying of the foundation (Ephesians 2:20). The four are right that the gifts ended and right that the signs confirmed messengers who spoke for God. They are wrong on the ground and wrong on the date. The text marks no end at the death of the last apostle; it marks one in Acts 28. The apostle did not leave the scene at that chapter: “Paul dwelt two whole years in his own hired house” (Acts 28:30), preaching and teaching, and the book records no sign in those years. If the signs belonged to the apostolic church as such, the record would show them while the apostle stood. It shows the last of them before the close, and it places a verdict on Israel’s leaders beside that close (Acts 28:25–28)."),
("p", "**Where the reading breaks the boundary.** Ephesians 2:20 says the household of God is “built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets”; it speaks of the building and states no date for signs. Schreiner’s ground takes a statement about the Body’s foundation and makes it the clock of Israel’s sign-program, and Gaffin, MacArthur and Thomas tie the same end to the apostolic office, which merges the program that ended with the ministry that goes on. **Verdict.** This date fails at Acts 28:30. The sign-program ended with the suspension of Israel’s program, and the apostle’s ministry did not end with it."),
("ctbl", "The objections and the verdicts.", [
    ("Objection", "Writers who state it", "Verdict"),
    ("Mark 16:17 promises signs to believers of every age", "Wimber; Brown", "Fails at the passage: the commission names the eleven, verse 20 is past tense, and Israel’s confirming signs are carried into the Body"),
    ("Joel 2:28 opens a program for all flesh", "Storms; Fee", "Fails at Joel 2:32 and Acts 2:20: the audience is Israel, the day is still coming, and Israel’s promise is carried into the Body"),
    ("Hebrews 2:4 says God gives gifts according to His will", "Deere; Grudem", "Strains at the sentence: the verbs are past, the readers are Hebrews, and a letter to Hebrews is made a charter for the Body"),
    ("John 20:30, 31 makes signs the means of belief", "Keener", "Strains at the stated purpose: the book carries the signs it selected for Israel’s confession, and credibility is not a program"),
    ("Scripture does not announce an end", "Deere; Brown; Storms", "Cannot stand in the text: it rests on silence and carries the sign-program across Acts 28 with no passage"),
    ("Believers report healings today", "Keener; Wimber; Brown", "Fails at the definition of a sign: a report is testimony, and calling it a Kingdom sign continues Israel’s program in the Body"),
    ("“That which is perfect” is the return of Christ", "Fee; Grudem; Storms; Carson", "Cannot stand in the verse: it states an end and not unbroken continuance, and carries the gifts past Acts 28 on silence"),
    ("The signs ended with the apostles, not at Acts 28", "Gaffin; MacArthur; Thomas; Schreiner", "Right on the end, wrong on the date: fails at Acts 28:30 and merges Israel’s sign-program with the apostle’s ministry"),
  ], [0.28, 0.22, 0.50]),
("sub", "What the volume rules"),
("bul", [
    "Seven objections from the continuationist reading fail at the passage each cites, and each carries Israel’s signs into the Body.",
    "No verse announces an end, and the position does not need one: it rests on the last recorded sign, the verdict in Acts 28 and the close of the book.",
    "The silence the objection faults in Acts is the silence its own conclusion rests on.",
    "Gaffin, MacArthur, Thomas and Schreiner are right that the miraculous gifts ended and wrong on the ground and the date.",
    "The passages on tongues, prophecy and healing in Paul’s letters are ruled on in Volumes 2 and 3.",
  ]),
]
